# Line endings are decided here rather than by each machine's core.autocrlf, so
# a checkout on Windows and the Linux checkout Vercel builds from are identical.
#
# LF everywhere. Every tool in this stack — Next, ESLint, TypeScript, VS Code —
# reads and writes LF happily on Windows, and pinning it means an editor or a
# script that writes LF no longer trips the "LF will be replaced by CRLF"
# warning on `git add`. There are no .bat/.cmd/.ps1 files here that would need
# CRLF; add an explicit `eol=crlf` line for them if that ever changes.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Assets git must never translate. `text=auto` already detects these, but being
# explicit keeps a stray heuristic from corrupting the logo files.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
